May be related to #1754 and the respective [GDCM issue](https://sourceforge.net/p/gdcm/bugs/551/).
It appears the failure (on my test system) starts once the decoded data is > 2^31 bytes, which makes me think there might be an i32 limitation somewhere.

Decoding with GDCM by itself is OK, SWIG/Python seems to be the cause.
From what I can tell GDCM defines `GetBuffer()` in [gdcmswig.i](https://github.com/malaterre/GDCM/blob/master/Wrapping/Python/gdcmswig.i) as:
```
%extend gdcm::Bitmap
{
  // http://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-list/2006-January/361540.html
  %cstring_output_allocate_size(char **buffer, unsigned int *size, free(*$1) );
  void GetBuffer(char **buffer, unsigned int *size) {
    *size = self->GetBufferLength();
    *buffer = (char*)malloc(*size);
    bool ret = self->GetBuffer(*buffer);
    if (!ret) {
      free(*buffer);
      *buffer = 0;
      *size = 0;
    }
  }
};
```
But the `%cstring_output_allocate_size()` macro wraps [this function](https://github.com/swig/swig/blob/b96b955ca15a01f0425fb26c234528530923202a/Lib/ocaml/ocamlrun.swg#L366), which takes an `int` rather than `uint`, so presumably any value above `2**31 - 1` is overflowing and so failing the `len < 0` check?
I don't actually know if this is fixable, as far as I can tell there isn't a way to use SWIG to return a str larger than 2**31 - 1.

Might be best to do an expected length check and if too large raise early. Alternatively, if the expected length is too large then split the decoding into multiple steps. Or just use the v3 backend 😛

